  "surface": "backend",
  "riskClass": "critical",
  "title": "auth-service internal routes have NO authentication middleware allowing any network-adjacent caller to create/delete users and assign roles",
  "description": "R6 deep audit finding. Dimension: auth_rbac. Severity: P0. Source SHA: bbd487a3. Scope: R6_DEEP_AUDIT_WAVE. The auth-service internal.ts router (line 30) has NO authentication middleware whatsoever. All 15+ endpoints (POST /users, DELETE /users/:id, POST /users/:id/roles, DELETE /users/:id/roles/:roleName, POST /roles, PATCH /roles/:id, DELETE /roles/:id, POST /users/verify) are fully open. While the intent is 'service-to-service only', there is no service token validation, no API key check, no IP allowlist — any caller that can reach the auth-service network port can create admin users, assign arbitrary roles, delete users, and verify credentials. In a Cloud Run deployment where services share the same VPC, any compromised service or misconfigured ingress exposes the entire user/role system.",
